BEGIN:VEVENT
DESCRIPTION:Source: IAS Princeton Physics Events\nSource URL: https://www.ias.edu/sns/events/physics\nEvent URL: https://www.ias.edu/sns/events/institute-advanced-study-multi-scale-initiative-3\nOnline URL: \nSpeaker: Tapio Schneider\, California Institute of Technology More: https://www.ias.edu/sns/events/institute-advanced-study-multi-scale-initiative-3 While climate change is certain\, precisely how climate will change is less clear. But advances in the accuracy of climate projections and in the quantification of their uncertainties are now within reach\, thanks to advances in the computational and data sciences and in the availability of Earth observations from space and from the ground. I will survey the design of a new Earth system model (ESM)\, developed by the Climate Modeling Alliance (CliMA)\, which is performance-portable across modern computing architectures. The talk will cover key new concepts and results\, including how data assimilation (DA) and machine learning (ML) can inform physics-based models with heterogeneous and noisy data and how substantial increases in the accuracy of simulations of uncertain processes can be achieved.\nAffiliation: \nLocation: Rubenstein Commons | Meeting Room 5\nScore: 100\nScore reasons: source_weight:55\; topic_bonus:+30:artificial intelligence\; institution_signal:+20\; local_bonus:+15\nExcerpt: Topic: First Principles\, Fast Algorithms: Physics-AI Synthesis in Earth System Modeling Speakers: Tapio Schneider\, California Institute of Technology More: https://www.ias.edu/sns/events/institute-advanced-study-multi-scale-initiative-3 Whi
DTEND:20260515T180000Z
LOCATION:Rubenstein Commons | Meeting Room 5
DTSTART:20260515T160000Z
SUMMARY:[AI] First Principles\, Fast Algorithms: Physics-AI Synthesis in Earth System Modeling — Tapio Schneider\, California Institute of Technology More: https://www.ias.edu/sns/events/institute-advanced-study-multi-scale-initiative-3 While climate change is certain\, precisely how climate will change is less clear. But advances in the accuracy of climate projections and in the quantification of their uncertainties are now within reach\, thanks to advances in the computational and data sciences and in the availability of Earth observations from space and from the ground. I will survey the design of a new Earth system model (ESM)\, developed by the Climate Modeling Alliance (CliMA)\, which is performance-portable across modern computing architectures. The talk will cover key new concepts and results\, including how data assimilation (DA) and machine learning (ML) can inform physics-based models with heterogeneous and noisy data and how substantial increases in the accuracy of simulations of uncertain processes can be achieved.
UID:55eadb4b38ce795dc8916953@tum-les-calendar
END:VEVENT
